e next webpage it's really hard to i  think even absorb as a kid you felt  you felt like the weird kid in class the  outsider in class did you  yes and i think i i still feel that way  you still feel that oh all the time  did you see yourself on the red carpet  at the football team where people were  you walking around they go it's amanda  gorman right here you still feel that  way yes i think there's like a little  bit of that imposter syndrome because  i'm such a nerd such a geek can be so  gangly and i think when i was growing up  you know i wasn't just kind of a really  brainy kid i was a really black kid i  was a really skinny kid i was really  small kid and also a kid whose voice was  kind of distorted by a speech impediment  so all of these things kind of made me  feel as othered or too different you  might say and so yeah it really made me  feel sometimes on the outside looking in  and i think  nowadays with more age behind me i look  back at it and i am so grateful for that  experience because all the things that  make me different make me who i am and  they make me great so starting from that  where does your confidence come from  today i think my confidence wasn't  necessarily something that i taught  myself but something that was modeled  for me i was raised by a single mother  and i think the way in which she would  walk through the space and kind of hold  herself as a black woman that was a  great type of kind of  symbol for me to look at and go ah this  is how i can continue in this world  gorman's walk through the world of  literature is really more like a sprint  that  is the promise to glade the hill we  climb if only we dare it her book the  hill we climb an inaugural poem for the  country became the first book of poetry  to debut at number one on usa today's  best-selling books list she says her new  book will serve as a record of the  baggage and the blessings we've carried  through this pandemic every cough seemed  catastrophe every person a po